[Music]  we're always accustomed to thinking of  inheritance as what happens in terms of  the nuclear genome that we inherit from  our mother and the nuclear genome that  we inherit from our father but what you  will see is that with mitochondria the  particular complement that is inherited  from your mother can be quite different  depending on the egg that is fertilized  so if we have a primordial egg cell and  here we have the nucleus of the egg cell  this particular egg may have let's say  three mitochondria in pink that carry  some sort of defect in their  mitochondrial genomes and then it may  have let's say six the equivalent of six  mitochondria that are perfectly healthy  what happens is in the process of  differentiation of maturation of eggs  when this undergoes cell division and  you end up with let's say two eggs you  can have a situation  where you have a distribution of  mitochondria where in this case the cell  on the right only received one of the  diseased mitochondria  while the cell on the left actually  received two what this means is that the  quantity the relative ratio of normal to  defective mitochondria is quite  different if you compare these two egg  cells so what this means is that  depending on which one is fertilized the  resulting embryo may have either a  significantly greater number of diseased  mitochondria or defective mitochondria  versus less and so this actually helps  determine whether or not the individual  in question  you know the resulting child that is  produced from that egg whether or not  that child will express the disease or  not express the disease or if it does  express the disease how severely  affected they might be by the  mitochondrial disease so what this means  is that mitochondria and their  distribution their ratio in a particular  egg cell in one woman can be quite  variable and depending on what egg is  fertilized and thus what embryo is then  developed you have a chance that you can  have a defect or you might not have a  defect at all with mitochondria  inherited diseases there's in fact a  threshold of defects at Carriker that  you have the inheritance of far more  than just two or three mitochondria you  have the inheritance of several  mitochondria and what we can see now are  situations in which let's say you have  three different eggs and I'm not going  to show the nuclear DNA because that's  not what we're interested right now you  might have one egg that has three of the  mitochondria that have the defect so in  pink another one just has one and  another one has two  let's say for the sake of simplicity all  of the other mitochondria I will use one  color to denote them let's do purple so  here we have another three another three  and that's for the sake of argument have  another three now if you compare these  three different eggs what you'll see is  that in this case 50% of the  mitochondria that that egg has are  defective right in this case we're down  to 25% are defective 1 pink 2 3 purple  and in this case we see that 40% are  defective what is observed is that  typically with many mitochondrial  diseases if you're in the range of 25%  to 40% being defective you still have  enough normal mitochondria to generate  enough ATP so quite often these  individuals are normal or healthy in  this case what we might have is a  situation where 50% or in fact what's  often the case 50% to 60% of  mitochondria having the defect can often  result in a disease presentation  so what this means is that you can have  siblings in the same family you might  have one sibling where the egg was  fertilized where the egg that was  fertilized had 25% of the mitochondria  carrying the defect while another egg  was fertilized for different sibling  that had 50% or more  this individual appear will show the  disease phenotype while this individual  will be entirely healthy and so this is  one of the remarkable observations with  mitochondrial disease is that even  within the same family even with  siblings you can see variations in  whether or not the disease is seen is  presented or not so that one way of  thinking about it is that there is a  threshold if you will of the  mitochondrial mutation that must be  present for the disease to display  itself so that interestingly enough you  may not have realised this but for every  egg that is fertilized it's a little bit  of sort of a one-armed bandit where you  pull the lever three things come up and  in some cases unfortunately if three  defects come up or a high enough  percentage of mitochondria are defective  you cross the threshold for disease so  this is a very different way of thinking  about how disease might be inherited not  through the nuclear genome but through  the mitochondrial genome  you  [Music]

